=== Design Inspirations ===

Markkit is an independent implementation. The following projects
inspired aspects of its design:

* Readability (Apache 2.0) – Mozilla's approach to content extraction
  informed our HTML-to-markdown pipeline design.

* Pandoc (GPL-2.0) – The universal document converter's format
  coverage and conversion philosophy inspired our multi-format
  architecture.

* turndown (MIT) – The JavaScript HTML-to-markdown converter's
  rule-based approach influenced our conversion engine design.

Markkit's parser, converter, and extraction pipeline are original
Rust implementations. This project is not affiliated with, endorsed
by, or derived from any of the above projects.

=== Key Dependencies ===

Markkit relies on permissively licensed crates, including:

* scraper (ISC), html5ever (MIT/Apache-2.0) – HTML parsing
* zip (MIT), quick-xml (MIT), calamine (MIT), cfb (MIT) – Office document containers and XML
* hayro-syntax / hayro (MIT/Apache-2.0) – PDF parsing and page rendering
* ocrs, rten (MIT/Apache-2.0) – optional Rust OCR backend
* reqwest (MIT/Apache-2.0), rustls (Apache-2.0/ISC/MIT) – optional HTTP client
* tokio (MIT) – async runtime
* serde / serde_json (MIT/Apache-2.0) – serialization
* pyo3 (MIT/Apache-2.0), napi (MIT), wasm-bindgen (MIT/Apache-2.0) – bindings

The allowed license set is enforced by `cargo deny check licenses` (deny.toml).
Binary packages (CLI, Python wheels, Node native addon, WebAssembly) statically
link these crates; their license texts and copyright notices are in each
crate's source on crates.io, pinned by Cargo.lock. cssparser and selectors
(via scraper) are MPL-2.0; their unmodified source is available from crates.io.

